# ── Dependencies & build artifacts (Vercel installs/serves; keep out of repo) ──
node_modules/
/package.json
/package-lock.json

# ── Dev / scratch ──
screenshots/
*.bak
fix_*.py
update_*.py
op_thread_check.js
*.log

# ── OS / editor ──
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
.vscode/
*.code-workspace

# ── Claude Code config — NOT part of the app ──
.claude/
# CLAUDE.md is TRACKED on purpose. It carries rules that cost real damage to learn —
# §2.8 (never reveal who supplies us, including via URLs, redirects and fallbacks) most
# of all — and while it was ignored those rules existed only on one machine, so any
# other session started without them.
CLAUDE (Rules Based).md
agents/
rules/
skills/
projects/
agent-memory/

# ── Secrets — never commit ──
.env
.env.local
.env.production
!.env.example

# Vercel project link — contains org/project IDs, and a stray one in a subfolder
# can re-point a deploy at the wrong directory.
.vercel/

# ── images/ is served PUBLICLY (Caddy's webroot is the repo; §2.5) ──
# The legacy static pages genuinely need images/*.jpg|png|webp, so the directory
# cannot be blocked wholesale. But nothing there is ever legitimately a document:
# a shipping label (real buyer address), a supplier catalogue, or a customer's
# embroidery file has no reason to be reachable from a browser.
# images/SS-Activewear-logo.png, images/otto-updatedlogo_0.png and a real USPS
# label PDF were tracked, deployed and returning 200 on the live apex — the first
# two name our suppliers outright, which is the §2.9 breach this blocks.
# Caddy 404s these extensions too; this is the half that stops them re-deploying.
images/*.pdf
images/*.PDF
images/*.pes
images/*.PES
images/*.dst
images/*.DST
images/*.emb
images/*.EMB
images/SS-Activewear-logo.png
images/otto-updatedlogo_0.png
